NNAAMMEE
       lesskey - customize key bindings for less

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS ((ddeepprreeccaatteedd))
       lleesssskkeeyy [[--oo oouuttppuutt]] [[----]] [[iinnppuutt]]
       lleesssskkeeyy [[----oouuttppuutt==oouuttppuutt]] [[----]] [[iinnppuutt]]
       lleesssskkeeyy --VV
       lleesssskkeeyy ----vveerrssiioonn

SSCCOOPPEE
       This document describes the format of the lleesssskkeeyy source file, which is
       used by lleessss version 582 and later.  In previous versions  of  lleessss,  a
       separate  program called lleesssskkeeyy was used to compile the lleesssskkeeyy source
       file into a format understood by lleessss.  This  compilation  step  is  no
       longer  required  and  the lleesssskkeeyy program is therefore deprecated, al‐
       though the file format remains supported by lleessss itself.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       A lleesssskkeeyy file specifies a set of key bindings  and  environment  vari‐
       ables to be used by subsequent invocations of lleessss.

FFIILLEE FFOORRMMAATT
       The  input  file consists of one or more _s_e_c_t_i_o_n_s.  Each section starts
       with a line that identifies the type  of  section.   Possible  sections
       are:

       #command
              Customizes command key bindings.

       #line-edit
              Customizes line-editing key bindings.

       #env   Defines environment variables.

       Blank lines and lines which start with a hash mark (#) are ignored, ex‐
       cept as noted below.

CCOOMMMMAANNDD SSEECCTTIIOONN
       The command section begins with the line

       #command

       If the command section is the first section in the file, this line  may
       be omitted.  The command section consists of lines of the form:

            _s_t_r_i_n_g <whitespace> _a_c_t_i_o_n [extra-string] <newline>

       Whitespace  is  any  sequence  of  one or more spaces and/or tabs.  The
       _s_t_r_i_n_g is the command key(s) which invoke the action.  The  _s_t_r_i_n_g  may
       be a single command key, or a sequence of up to 15 keys.  The _a_c_t_i_o_n is
       the name of the less action, from the list below.   The  characters  in
       the  _s_t_r_i_n_g may appear literally, or be prefixed by a caret to indicate
       a control key.  A backslash followed by one to three octal  digits  may
       be  used  to  specify a character by its octal value.  A backslash fol‐
       lowed by certain characters specifies input characters as follows:

            l  l  l.   \b   BACKSPACE (0x08)  \e   ESCAPE    (0x1B)  \n   NEW‐
            LINE   (0x0A) \r   RETURN    (0x0D) \t   TAB  (0x09)

            \k  followed by a single character represents the char(s) produced
            when one of these keys is pressed:

            l  l.   \kb  BACKSPACE  (the  BACKSPACE  key)  \kB  ctrl-BACKSPACE
            \kd  DOWN  ARROW  \kD  PAGE  DOWN  \ke  END  \kh  HOME \ki  INSERT
            \kl  LEFT ARROW \kL  ctrl-LEFT ARROW \kr  RIGHT  ARROW  \kR  ctrl-
            RIGHT  ARROW  \kt  BACKTAB  \ku  UP ARROW \kU  PAGE UP \kx  DELETE
            \kX  ctrl-DELETE \k1  F1


            A backslash followed by any other character indicates that charac‐
            ter  is  to be taken literally.  Characters which must be preceded
            by backslash include caret, space, tab, hash mark  and  the  back‐
            slash itself.

            An  action may be followed by an "extra" string.  When such a com‐
            mand is entered while running lleessss, the action is  performed,  and
            then  the  extra  string is parsed, just as if it were typed in to
            lleessss.  This feature can be used in certain  cases  to  extend  the
            functionality  of  a  command.   For example, see the "{" and ":t"
            commands in the example below.  The extra  string  has  a  special
            meaning for the "quit" action: when lleessss quits, the ASCII value of
            the first character of the extra string is used as its  exit  sta‐
            tus.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEE
